  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 314 AM CST Sat Nov 7 2026\n\nMostly quiet weather is anticipated for the next several days. A\nzonal flow pattern across the CONUS will prevail until mid-week\nwhen synoptic models indicate a vigorous short-wave trough will\ndig a significant upper trough over the Great Lakes/Ohio River \nValley. As the flow becomes increasingly northwesterly, a cold\nfront will drop into central Illinois by late Wednesday. Despite\nthe approaching boundary and the presence of strong upper\ndynamics, deep-layer moisture will be lacking. The 00z Nov 7 GFS\nshows precipitable water values increasing to just 0.50 to 0.75\nahead of the front...while the better moisture with values in\nexcess of 1 inch remain well to the south from the Ozarks\nsouthward. The latest NBM brings 20 PoPs for showers into the\npicture Wednesday night into Thursday and this seems reasonable\ngiven the current GFS/ECMWF/GEM consensus. Once the front passes\nand upper heights fall, another shot of chilly fall air will be\nin store by the end of the week. After highs in the 60s for much\nof the week, readings will drop back below normal in the lower\nto middle 50s Thursday through next Saturday. In fact, the 00z\nLREF indicates a high probability (70-80% chance) of highs below\n50 degrees on Friday.",
